{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS introduces additional policy details to access denied error messages - AWS","context":"AWS has updated access denied error messages in scenarios where the resources are within the same account and organization. The new error messages include the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the specific IAM and AWS Organizations policy responsible for the denied access. This enhancement allows for faster identification and resolution of access denied issues, particularly in cases where multiple policies of the same type are involved. The error message now provides the ARN for various policy types, including Service Control Policies (SCP), Resource Control Policies (RCP), identity-based policies, session policies, and permission boundaries. This new feature will be rolled out across all AWS services and regions. For more information, refer to the IAM documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/additional-policy-details-access-denied-error/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon S3 adds additional policy details to access denied error messages - AWS","context":"Amazon S3 has updated its access denied error messages to include the specific AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) and AWS Organizations policy Amazon Resource Name (ARN) for same-account and same-organization requests. This enhancement allows users to easily identify the exact policy responsible for a denied request and remediate the issue directly. Previously, S3 access denied error messages only included the policy type and reason for denial, making it necessary to manually inspect each policy to determine the root cause when multiple policies of the same type existed. The new capability applies to all AWS Regions, including AWS GovCloud (US) and AWS China Regions. Users can refer to the S3 User Guide and IAM troubleshooting documentation for more information on how to troubleshoot access denied errors in Amazon S3","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/s3-additional-policy-details-access-denied-error-messages/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-messaging-and-streaming","skill_description":"Guides general use of AWS messaging and streaming services. Covers Amazon SQS, Amazon SNS, Amazon EventBridge, Amazon MQ, Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, Amazon Data Firehose, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK).  Use when reasoning about messaging and streaming patterns. Also identifies which AWS service owns each customer communication channel: email (Amazon SES), and WhatsApp, SMS, MMS, RCS, voice and mobile push (the AWS End User Messaging family of services). Routes the request to the specialized skill for that channel. Defers to the channel's specialized skill when the user already named a specific channel. In general, use specific skills or documentation searches for detailed service-specific questions. Do NOT use for MSK or Managed Service for Apache Flink questions, prefer specific skills. Does not configure customer communication channels; defers to specific skills.\n\n\nServices: sqs, sns, eventbridge, mq, kinesis, kds, firehose, flink, msf, msk, kafka, ses, socialmessaging, pinpoint-sms-voice-v2\nTasks: learn, architect, debug, deploy\nPersona: developer, architect, devops\nWorkload: serverless, event-driven, streaming, data-analytics, messaging\n*","skill_name":"aws-messaging-and-streaming"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-cleanrooms","skill_description":"Troubleshoots and debugs AWS Clean Rooms collaboration issues related to IAM roles, S3 bucket policies, KMS keys, Lake Formation permissions, and CloudWatch logging for custom ML model training and inference jobs. Use when a customer reports permission failures, access errors, or log publishing issues in Clean Rooms.\n\nServices: cleanrooms, cleanrooms-ml, iam, s3, kms, lakeformation, cloudwatch\nTasks: debug, troubleshoot, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, analyst\nWorkload: data-collaboration, ml\n*","skill_name":"aws-cleanrooms"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up AWS docs on Q Business ChatSync access denied causes"}

